@@ -69,3 +69,8 @@ Backend integration tests run the real API (`dotnet Competency.Api.dll` from the
 - SQL-command budget on a throwaway Postgres with `log_statement=all`: Npgsql resets a pooled connection with `DISCARD ALL`, logged as a `statement:` line after every command, so the raw count is double. Count application commands only (drop `DISCARD ALL` and the readiness probe line); dump the statements of one request to see repeats before judging the budget.
 - A test that is red because of a code defect cannot prove its own mutants (every mutant is "killed" by the standing failure). Prove them on a copy where the defect is patched as the first of the runner's `edits` (here `<Link>` to `<a>` in a modal), and check that patch alone is green; mutants that still survive then need their own test.
 - antd `modal.confirm/error` content is rendered from the `App` context, outside `RouterProvider` when `Providers` wraps the router, so a `<Link>` in it throws `useContext(...) is null`. Mount the hook under the same `Providers > RouterProvider` as production; `MemoryRouter` inside `Providers` hides the crash.
+- Connection limit of the shared container: the default `max_connections=100` runs out in a full run (dozens of class hosts migrate and pool at once), and the symptom is random classes red with `53300: sorry, too many clients already` or a bare 500 from a host, while each class is green alone. Prove it by sampling `docker exec <pg container> psql -U postgres -tAc "select count(*) from pg_stat_activity where backend_type = 'client backend'"` every 2 s next to the run (find the container by `docker ps --filter ancestor=postgres:18.6-trixie`, skip the dev stack's `competency-dev-db-1`). `.WithCommand("-c", "max_connections=500")` on the builder appends to the module's own `-c fsync=off` (`show fsync` stayed off); a pool cap per host would break the held-lock tests that count waiters.
+- Guard versus fail-first: a perf rewrite keeps behaviour, so its new tests are green on the pre-fix copy and only mutations prove them; say so in the plan. Run the pre-fix copy first with every new test, sort the reds (fixes) from the greens (guards), then aim one mutant at each branch the rewrite introduced (first-only check, status filter dropped, kind constant swapped, version zeroed, per-type cache key). A mutant that survives on the existing classes (the per-type cache key of the compiled visibility filter survived 60 tests) is a missing pin, not a nuisance.
+- Order the assertions of a test that changes state so every status assert comes before the next `Expect(OK)` that depends on that state: a mutant that wrongly accepts the first request moves the entity tag and the later `Expect(OK)` then fails with 412, which hides the property under test.
+- Arrangements worth reusing: `MatrixKit`'s track is a draft (the admin creates it, nothing approves it), so the superior approver's read of it is testable without extra setup, and the matrix direction is always active (a matrix is created only on an active one), so a draft direction never reaches an approval route. A block written by the head above for the matrix unit is a draft; its read by the unit's head is the head-visible case. A scoped draft block outside the responsible HR's units: assign an HR on the team itself (units = the team), grant the team head the HR role after the head made a draft competency, let that head create the block for the department with the competency in it, and flip the responsible HR's roles between two identical accept requests as the control (409 NoRight, then 200).
+- A position linked to a draft track of one unit, listed on the department's list, shows the track name to that unit's head and hides it from a sibling head (refusal text of the position matrix and `linkedTrack` of `matrix-positions`); two blocker kinds in one publication preview (a draft direction whose name an active one took, plus a level with no position) exercise the per-type cache of the visibility filter.
